00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:10.019
September 9th, 1976.

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:17.070
The day, Mao Zedong died, three decades after
founding the People’s Republic of China.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.340
Few countries are so easily divided into two
distinct eras.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:26.500
With his death, the economic walls around
the Red Dragon slowly fell, allowing the largest

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.500
population on earth access to the world economy.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.489
In 1978, there were, essentially zero private
companies.

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:42.519
All were state-owned, with only a tiny 140,000
people, or 0.01% of its population, employed

00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:43.649
privately.

00:00:43.649 --> 00:00:48.160
Just three years later, thanks to Mao’s
successor, Deng Xiaoping, that number was

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.789
already 16 times higher.

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:55.930
China in the ‘80s and ‘90s was the golden
opportunity to start a company.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.780
Big, inefficient state-owned corporations
had left huge gaps in the economy, people

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.870
were moving to the city, finally, with money
to spend, and the government was eager to

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.670
encourage enterprise.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:12.030
Which is to say, Ren Zhengfei couldn’t have
picked a better time than 1987 to start his

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.790
company, called Huawei!

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:18.350
A few years later, it won a contract with
the People’s Liberation Army, which, although

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:24.140
small in financial terms, was huge for winning
favor with the Communist Party, who then protected

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.400
the company from competition.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.710
With this help, Huawei became, today, the
2nd largest phone company in the world, just

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.160
ahead of Apple, and behind only Samsung.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.980
Up until just a few months ago, it was expected
to pass Samsung to become number one, lead

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:45.600
the world in the spread of 5G and gain even
more market share than its current 29% of

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.200
all telecommunications equipment on earth.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.870
Then, with one, swift signature, Huawei was
banned.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:58.110
Not only does this stop the company from selling
equipment in the U.S., but all Huawei products,

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:02.730
everywhere are banned from working with American
companies, who supply Huawei with components

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:06.370
like Intel chips, and software like the Google
Play Store.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:11.530
So, is Huawei, as the United States claims,
a real threat to national security?

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.280
A trojan horse for the Chinese government?

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.720
Or is the ban merely political?

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.590
The problem with most answers to these questions
is that they focus only on the technology,

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.450
the politics, or China.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:29.460
But to really understand Huawei, you have
to marry all three.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.380
Who really owns a company?

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.110
It’s a pretty reasonable question and should
have a pretty simple answer.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:40.850
You don’t usually need to go around reading
Articles of Incorporation, just some common

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:41.850
sense.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:46.420
A few decent clues are whoever’s office
is on the 100th floor, or, maybe, whoever’s

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.330
business card is most expensive.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:51.620
But not always.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.959
Because, of course, the structure of a company
is not designed for our legibility, but, rather,

00:02:56.959 --> 00:02:59.670
to make more money or limit somebody’s power.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:04.880
So, you see lots of weird subsidiaries, holding
companies without employees based in the Virgin

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.069
Islands, deliberately confusing names, and
dual-class shares.

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:10.950
Things can get… weird.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:17.060
Take John, for example, of Papa fame, who,
facing public pressure, resigned as the company

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:18.060
chairman.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.280
A few weeks later, John decided actually,
he’d like to return to his pizza Papa position.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:24.870
The board said, “Yeeeah…

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.120
Hard pass!” and changed the company’s
bylaws to block their own largest shareholder

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.480
from taking over the company he founded.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.090
He then sued his own company.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:39.740
One time, a pharmaceutical company called
Rockwell Medical split into two legal corporations,

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:44.319
each claiming to be the real, legitimate Rockwell
Medical Incorporated with the same address

00:03:44.319 --> 00:03:49.320
and same shareholders, leaving everyone wondering,
who’s the real pope?

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.240
The point is, it’s actually not that simple
of a question.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:57.209
And, that’s before you add the whole Socialism
twist.

00:03:57.209 --> 00:04:01.600
If you do ask Huawei who owns the company,
they’ll happily take you to a special room

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.460
in their Shenzhen headquarters, where a giant
blue book, because little and red were taken,

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.420
sits behind glass.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.650
Exactly the way you’d expect a multi-billion
dollar tech company to keep its records.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:17.060
In it, Huawei says, is a list of every person
who owns shares in the company.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.400
And, as they’ll enthusiastically assure
you, none of these people are the Chinese

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.410
government.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.320
Case… not closed, obviously.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.240
Here’s what we know:

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:33.990
Huawei Technology LLC is 100% owned by a holding
company called Huawei Investment &amp; Holding,

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.710
which is itself owned by two parties:

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:42.970
Huawei’s CEO owns about 1%, and the rest,
the Union of Huawei Investment &amp; Holding.

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:48.040
It says this is only for legal reasons, and,
I’ll remind you, convoluted ownership structures

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.090
aren’t at all unusual.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:55.150
For all practical purposes, Huawei claims,
the company is owned by its employees - the

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.000
names in the blue book - who own stock in
the company.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.520
But, again, it’s not so simple.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:06.560
What Huawei calls stock is, actually, apologizes
in advance to non-business majors, Synthetic

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:12.130
Equity, or, for maximum, put-it-in-the-parking-lot,
synergize, circle-back jargon-points, Restricted

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:13.720
Phantom Shares.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:14.720
OoOooOoo…

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.460
Phantom… spooky sounding!

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:22.150
It’s a lot like regular equity - shareholders,
in this case, Huawei employees, receive a

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:25.240
share of the company’s profits - a dividend.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:29.780
They stand to make money, so they’re incentivized
to add value to the company.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:33.690
Except it’s “synthetic” - as in not
totally… real.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:39.240
It can’t be sold or transferred, doesn’t
give much say in the operations of the company,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:44.479
and, if an employee, for any reason, leaves
the company, he or she must also sell back

00:05:44.479 --> 00:05:45.840
their shares.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:51.010
As two American researchers summarize “…this
virtual stock ownership has nothing to do

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:52.990
with financing or control.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:56.259
It is purely a profit-sharing incentive scheme.”

00:05:56.259 --> 00:06:01.370
When we ask the question “Who owns Huawei?”,
what we’re really asking is who, if they

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:06.030
really wanted the company to do something,
like, say, spy on foreign countries, has the

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.479
power to do so.

00:06:07.479 --> 00:06:12.800
The answer, it seems, is the union, who owns
the holding company, who owns the company.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.510
So, who controls the union?

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:20.790
This time the answer is much easier: Under
Chinese labor law, Unions are subject to their

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:26.770
superior branch - local Unions answer to provincial
unions, all the way up through a winding python

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:31.800
of hierarchy, ending at the chairman of the
All-China Federation of Trade Unions, who

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.110
also sits on the National People's Congress.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:39.490
Unions in China are, therefore, largely an
extension of the Communist Party.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:44.460
Now, you’d be right to point out that this
alone shouldn’t be enough to ban an entire

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.070
company from doing business with an entire
country.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:52.480
After all, this logic applies to many Chinese
companies, not just Huawei.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.580
It would be ridiculous to ban all of them.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:07:01.130
To that, defenders of the ban would say, But
Huawei is no ordinary company; it has a long

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:03.080
history of alleged misbehavior.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:09.560
It’s CFO was, quite prominently, arrested
in Canada on the grounds of violating US sanctions

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:10.740
on Iran.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:16.229
Cisco accused the company of copying their
manuals after finding the same typos in Huawei’s,

00:07:16.229 --> 00:07:18.610
which its CEO said was just a coincidence.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:24.400
The CIA also claims it has evidence that Huawei
has taken money from Chinese intelligence

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:25.400
services.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:32.850
And finally, in 2012, China gave the African
Union a new $200 million headquarters in Ethiopia.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:37.639
Then, last year, a French newspaper quoted
anonymous technicians in the building who

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:42.919
claimed they caught Huawei equipment copying
and sending data to servers in Shanghai - which

00:07:42.919 --> 00:07:46.520
China and the African Union officially deny.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:51.389
On one hand, there’s a lot of smoke here,
on the other, there’s also a lot of people

00:07:51.389 --> 00:07:54.850
who have a strong interest in you believing
there’s a fire.

00:07:54.850 --> 00:08:00.410
All of these incidents are alleged, and, neither
the UK nor the US has ever presented clear

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:05.550
evidence of a backdoor in Huawei equipment,
despite having many years to investigate.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:11.569
Before the ban, before its phones were cut-off
from Google services, before it lost its relationship

00:08:11.569 --> 00:08:17.789
with ARM, before it was, indirectly, forced
to sell off its undersea cable business, Huawei

00:08:17.789 --> 00:08:19.690
was unstoppable.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:24.610
Experts tend to agree that it’s years ahead
of anyone else on 5G technology and that its

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:27.700
equipment is, in general, significantly cheaper.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:32.909
That’s why, despite pressure from its allies,
Britain has continued buying Huawei equipment

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:37.089
- doing so saves, at least, millions of dollars.

00:08:37.089 --> 00:08:42.310
It mirrors the predicament many countries
around the world face with Chinese investment:

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:47.040
Even knowing the risk of dependence on China,
can they resist the economic benefits?

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:52.330
It’s no secret that, while this is going
on, the U.S. is in a trade war with China,

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:57.800
and banning Huawei gives it both negotiating
leverage and protects American telecommunications

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:02.240
companies from foreign competition, exactly
at its strongest.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.450
So, make no mistake: The ban is political.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:10.410
It’s been strongly hinted as being on the
table for trade negotiations.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:14.459
And it’s quite possible the ban will soon
be reversed if a deal is made.

00:09:14.459 --> 00:09:19.110
But, it’s just as important to note that
just because something is strategically motivated,

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:22.970
doesn’t mean there aren’t, other, perfectly
legitimate arguments.

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:26.840
Much of the confusion around this issue is
centered around the fact that politicians

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.870
can agree on the same policy for vastly different
reasons.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:36.470
The truth is you don’t need to make a judgment
call on whether you trust Huawei or its CEO.

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:41.510
You only need to recognize the most basic
feature of the Chinese system: everything

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:43.700
is the concern of the state.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:49.200
From what is shown on TV, to who is allowed
to travel, even how long you can play Fornite,

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.770
and certainly the movements of its largest
corporations, the Communist Party is everywhere,

00:09:54.770 --> 00:10:00.350
if not now, and quite possibly, not, in the
case of Huawei, eventually.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:05.660
Having an enemy is, unfortunately, useful
for American politicians, especially one as

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:07.820
misunderstood as China,

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:12.680
but so too is it crucial for a country to
keep its communications infrastructure, through

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:17.830
which national secrets are shared and information
wars, increasingly fought, free from foreign

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:19.220
influence.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:24.790
This is much bigger than any one company,
bigger than who controls 5G, bigger even than

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:25.790
the trade-war.

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:29.530
It’s really about the Chinese system being
used against it.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:35.040
The story of Huawei is very much the story
of China - both only realized their full potential

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:39.750
upon opening up to the world’s markets,
where Huawei now makes the much of its revenue,

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:43.920
and how China was turned into one of the most
powerful nations on earth.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:49.709
Huawei is what it is today to a significant
degree because of its close relationship with

00:10:49.709 --> 00:10:52.399
and subsequent protection by the Chinese government.

00:10:52.399 --> 00:10:58.209
Now, it’s a victim of that same closeness
- the other side of that very profitable coin,

00:10:58.209 --> 00:11:03.769
which, ironically, has long blocked many western
tech companies - Google, Facebook, Twitter,

00:11:03.769 --> 00:11:09.450
YouTube… - from operating in China on, you
guessed it… national security grounds.

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:14.240
China would like the benefits of free, open
trade abroad, while closely protecting and

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.810
controlling business domestically.

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:19.339
But now it’s forced to chose.

00:11:19.339 --> 00:11:23.910
In a trade war like this one, each side is
trying to use game theory to interpret and

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:27.060
outmaneuver their opponent, who may or may
not be lying.

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:32.089
For example, say there are two types of people
- knights, who always tell the truth, and

00:11:32.089 --> 00:11:33.920
knaves, who always lie.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:39.160
You say “If I asked if you were a knight,
what would you say”? and they respond “No”,

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:41.300
which are they - knight or knave?

